Consider this synchronous C# code:

```csharp
IEnumerable<string> Lines()
{
    try
    {
        Console.WriteLine("open");
        yield return "a";
        yield return "b";
    }
    finally
    {
        Console.WriteLine("closed");
    }
}

foreach (var line in Lines())
{
    Console.WriteLine(line);
    break;
}
```

The output is `open`, `a`, `closed`. The iterator body never reaches `yield return "b"` or the end of its `try` block. Explain how `closed` still gets printed after the `break`.

Use this iterator:

```csharp
IEnumerable<string> Lines()
{
    try
    {
        yield return "a";
        yield return "b";
    }
    finally
    {
        Console.WriteLine("closed");
    }
}
```

A consumer runs the following code instead of a `foreach`. It never calls `MoveNext()` again and never calls `Dispose()`. The program then continues with other work.

```csharp
var e = Lines().GetEnumerator();
e.MoveNext();
Console.WriteLine(e.Current);
```

Which statement correctly describes what happens to `closed`?
